1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,801
On the ninth of March, 1974, a
sixty-one-year-old bookseller flew from

2
00:00:04,801 --> 00:00:09,373
Japan to a small island in the
Philippines, walked into the jungle, and

3
00:00:09,373 --> 00:00:13,545
read a short statement aloud to a man
standing at attention in a patched

4
00:00:13,545 --> 00:00:14,924
uniform.

5
00:00:14,974 --> 00:00:19,379
The statement said that the Japanese
Fourteenth Area Army had been dissolved.

6
00:00:19,379 --> 00:00:23,937
That all combat activity was to cease
immediately. That the man was relieved of

7
00:00:23,937 --> 00:00:25,363
his duties.

8
00:00:25,413 --> 00:00:30,748
The man listening was Second Lieutenant
Hiroo Onoda. He was fifty-one years old.

9
00:00:30,748 --> 00:00:35,870
He had been fighting the Second World War,
alone, for the last eighteen months, and

10
00:00:35,870 --> 00:00:39,581
with others for the twenty-seven years
before that.

11
00:00:40,131 --> 00:00:45,911
He had heard, many times, that the war was
over. He had read leaflets saying so. He

12
00:00:45,911 --> 00:00:50,312
had heard it from loudspeakers, from
search parties, from his own family's

13
00:00:50,312 --> 00:00:55,541
voices played over the jungle on a
recording. He had dismissed all of it.

14
00:00:55,591 --> 00:01:00,754
What he had been waiting for was this. An
order, from the officer who gave him the

15
00:01:00,754 --> 00:01:03,712
original one, delivered in person.

16
00:01:04,262 --> 00:01:09,556
That officer was a bookseller now. He had
been found in a shop in Japan and asked to

17
00:01:09,556 --> 00:01:11,842
put the uniform back on.

18
00:01:12,692 --> 00:01:16,412
When he finished reading, Onoda took the
bolt out of his rifle.

19
00:01:18,912 --> 00:01:24,080
Hiroo Onoda was born in March 1922, in a
village in Wakayama Prefecture. Before the

20
00:01:24,080 --> 00:01:29,195
war he worked for a trading company in
China, selling lacquerware. He was good at

21
00:01:29,195 --> 00:01:34,309
it. He had a gift for languages and an
ordinary young man's life, and when he was

22
00:01:34,309 --> 00:01:38,697
drafted at twenty he was, by every
measure, unremarkable.

